1
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,500
The most interesting part of Hold the Box
happened when we saw it in other people's hands.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,200
First they try to coordinate.
Then they blame each other.

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,500
Then they laugh.
Then they try again.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,300
And when they finally reach the finish,
they celebrate together.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,200
That's when we knew the idea worked.

6
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:24,300
Because the sync left the screen
and moved into the people.

7
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,800
It looks like a game about carrying a box.

8
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:53,000
But Hold the Box is really a game about sync.

9
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,800
Two players. One keyboard.
And one chance to get there together.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,800
Hold the Box is a local co-op game
for two players.

11
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,200
You both share a single load.

12
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:10,500
Every jump, every step, and every mistake
hits the other player directly.

13
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:14,500
When you work together, you move faster
and control the box better.

14
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:17,000
When you fall out of sync,
stability disappears — and the trouble starts.

15
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:18,600
The enemy isn't the map.
It's what you're carrying.

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,500
Across the campaign, players cross four maps
in three different environments:

17
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:28,800
sea, jungle, and desert.

18
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:34,800
Along the way: rain, wind, waves, NPCs,
and hazards that test your coordination.

19
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,300
The stability system reacts
to every decision you make.

20
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:41,500
Jump together, and the box stays stable.

21
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:45,500
Split too far, or land at different times,
and it starts to tilt.

22
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,300
When the balance breaks,
the consequences show up fast.

23
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,000
Each kind of box changes
how you have to cooperate.

24
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,500
The VIP box needs precision.
The cat box needs patience.

25
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:03,500
And the bomb box needs control.

26
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,500
The objectives change,
but they all ask the same question:

27
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:09,200
How well can you stay in sync?

28
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:20,500
Hold the Box was made during GameJamPlus 2026
under the theme Better Together.

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,300
We wanted the simplest take possible:

30
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,800
an experience where progress depends less
on running fast —
and more on learning to move together.

31
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:35,500
We don't play the same games anymore.
But making a game was the best excuse
to do it together again.

32
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Hold the Box.
Play it with two.
